```typescript
export class LRUCache<T> {
  private cache = new Map<string, { value: T; expiresAt: number }>()
  private maxSize: number
  constructor(maxSize: number = 100) { this.maxSize = maxSize }
  get(key: string): T | undefined {
    const entry = this.cache.get(key)
    if (!entry) return undefined
    if (Date.now() > entry.expiresAt) { this.cache.delete(key); return undefined }
    this.cache.delete(key); this.cache.set(key, entry)
    return entry.value
  }
  set(key: string, value: T, ttlMs: number = 60000): void {
    if (this.cache.size >= this.maxSize) {
      const firstKey = this.cache.keys().next().value
      if (firstKey !== undefined) this.cache.delete(firstKey)
    }
    this.cache.set(key, { value, expiresAt: Date.now() + ttlMs })
  }
}
```

Key points:
1. It's a generic in-memory LRU cache with TTL support.
2. Uses a JS `Map` which preserves insertion order — the oldest-inserted key is first.
3. Eviction strategy: when the cache is full (size >= maxSize, default 100), it evicts the *first* key in the Map — i.e., the least recently inserted/used key. Because `get()` re-inserts the accessed key (delete + set), the Map order reflects recency of access, making it a true LRU. Note: `set()` on an existing key doesn't delete first, so re-setting an existing key doesn't refresh its recency position — a subtle caveat. Actually, `set` on an existing key updates the value in place, keeping its original position. So the LRU ordering is based on `get` recency and insertion order, not `set` recency for existing keys.
4. Expiration: lazy expiration — entries store `expiresAt` (set time + ttlMs, default 60s). On `get`, if `Date.now() > expiresAt`, the entry is deleted and `undefined` returned. Expired entries are only purged when accessed; they still occupy space until evicted or read. Also note: expired entries are not checked during `set`, so an expired entry could be evicted as "LRU" anyway.
5. Edge case: if maxSize is 0... `set` would evict firstKey (undefined if empty) then set. Fine.

